Chronic Kidney Disease (Salt-Wasting Nephropathy)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Renal Loss
Obligatory polyuria, Frequent postural hypotension, Persistently elevated fractional excretion of sodium

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-010
Reason for Visit: Frequent episodes of dizziness upon standing, profound lethargy, and unexplained weight loss despite adequate oral intake, requiring urgent after-hours evaluation to assess volume status and prevent syncopal trauma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and live-in caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted recurrent, severe postural dizziness resulting in a near-fall earlier in the evening, combined with chronic profound fatigue. This indicated significant hemodynamic compromise secondary to chronic volume depletion, necessitating an urgent home visit to assess cardiovascular stability and prevent acute injury prior to the next routine in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Constant dizziness when standing, exhaustion, and passing too much urine. Patient states, I feel completely drained, and every time I stand up, the room goes black for a few seconds.
History of Present Illness: The patient, who has a known history of chronic tubulointerstitial kidney disease, reports worsening obligatory polyuria over the past several months that is unresponsive to mild fluid restriction. There is chronic excessive thirst, though the patient struggles to keep up with fluid losses. Over the past week, the patient has experienced frequent episodes of volume depletion with severe postural hypotension. Muscle cramps, likely secondary to electrolyte imbalances, occur nightly in the calves. The patient reports chronic fatigue, severe lethargy, and an unintentional weight loss of 3kg over the last month due to a chronic fluid deficit. Interestingly, the patient notes a strong, specific craving for salt.
Functional Status: Functionally limited; reliant on caregiver for mobility assistance due to a high risk of syncope upon standing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of falls and resulting trauma due to pronounced orthostatic hypotension.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg (Supine) -> 95/60 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 85 bpm -> 110 bpm (Standing) | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 64.0 Kg
General: Appears chronically ill, pale, and frail. Rises to stand extremely slowly and requires immediate stabilization.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Normal heart sounds. Significant orthostatic hypotension is objectively documented, with a concurrent compensatory tachycardia upon standing, confirming severe intravascular volume depletion. Respiratory: Lungs are clear. Genitourinary/Hydration: The clinical picture reflects the diagnostic triad: obligatory polyuria is reported, frequent postural hypotension is confirmed, and persistently elevated fractional excretion of sodium is strongly suspected given the underlying renal pathology. Examination reveals poor skin turgor, markedly dry mucous membranes, and a total absence of peripheral edema, typical of salt-wasting states rather than fluid-retaining renal failure.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, but the bathroom is distant from the bedroom, increasing the risk of a fall during frequent nocturia.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is not on loop or thiazide diuretics that could mimic this presentation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Kidney Disease (Salt-Wasting Nephropathy) - Status: Exacerbated/Decompensated
Differentials:

Adrenal Insufficiency (Addison's Disease) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of hyperpigmentation, lack of prominent gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea/vomiting), and a clear preexisting history of chronic tubulointerstitial renal disease explaining the pathology.

Diuretic Abuse/Overuse - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Careful medication reconciliation confirming no access or prescription for diuretic therapies.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of jugular venous distension, lack of peripheral edema, clear lung fields, and a presentation dominated by severe volume depletion rather than fluid overload.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, uncompensated intravascular volume depletion secondary to a known chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathy, resulting in a classic salt-wasting syndrome. The inability of the damaged renal tubules to reabsorb sodium and water leads to obligatory polyuria and profound orthostatic hypotension. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried a very high, immediate risk of the patient suffering a syncopal fall resulting in severe trauma (e.g., hip fracture, head injury) during nighttime ambulation to the bathroom. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to accurately diagnose the hemodynamic instability, institute immediate fall-prevention strategies, and advise on acute dietary sodium and fluid loading to safely bridge the patient to formal nephrology review. The expected investigation results include persistently elevated fractional excretion of sodium despite clinical volume depletion and low serum sodium that would assist in confirming the diagnosis during specialist follow-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised the immediate consumption of a high-sodium broth and aggressive oral fluid intake. Educated the caregiver on strict postural management techniques.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce your dizzy spells and regain stability, commit to a strict "two-stage standing" routine. First, sit on the edge of the bed for a full 60 seconds. Then, stand up holding onto a solid object for another 30 seconds before walking. When you successfully complete this without the room going dark, consciously recognize that your deliberate patience prevented a dangerous fall. This immediate feeling of safety will reinforce your new habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for a home occupational therapy assessment to install grab bars and provide a bedside commode.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode heavily recommended to reduce nighttime transit risks.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for UEC (Urea, Electrolytes, Creatinine) panel and adjustment of baseline fluid/sodium prescriptions; subsequent Nephrology review required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call an ambulance or proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if you experience a complete loss of consciousness, if you fall and strike your head or experience severe pain, or if you develop new-onset chest pain or severe shortness of breath.

